   "transcript": "Prof Jonathan Bate: One of the wonderful things about Shakespeare is that he leaves all sorts of fingerprints of his background in his work, so for instance in his comedy The Merry Wives of Windsor there’s a wonderful scene that offers a Latin lesson. There’s a Welsh schoolmaster and we know that Shakespeare had a Welsh schoolmaster here in Stratford, and there’s a clever cheeky boy who’s being given a grammar lesson and the boy – surprise, surprise – is called William.\nInterviewer: Is there any way of telling whether he enjoyed it, enjoyed school?\nProf Jonathan Bate: Well, does any little boy enjoy getting up at 6 o’clock in the morning and studying 12 hours of Latin grammar and syntax every day? That said, when it actually came to studying the literature that was available in the upper forms, Shakespeare was clearly very excited. His imagination was fired by the poetry, the stories of the great poets of ancient Rome.\nInterviewer: Can we tell what he read at school?\nProf Jonathan Bate: One of the key texts was by the great humanist educator Erasmus, and it would teach you the art of using language in clever and varied ways. So, for example, an exercise Shakespeare would have done would have begun with the boys saying greetings in Latin, Salve, and then greetings to you, Salve et tu. Then greetings to your father, Salve et tu pater, making it more and more complicated and then maybe throwing in a bit of comedy to keep it lively for the boys. Greetings to you, you who fills the bottomless pit of your stomach with cake and indulges in an excess of ale. And you suddenly see there the language is becoming like that of Shakespeare’s comedies, the language of Falstaff for example.\nInterviewer: If he hadn’t had this grammar school education, could he still have become the William Shakespeare that we know today?\nProf Jonathan Bate: I really don’t think he could. I think there’s an absolutely symbiotic relationship between the Tudor educational revolution, the great expansion of the number of grammar schools in the 16th century, and the literary renaissance of the Elizabethan era. That’s to say the grammar schools were founded for the rather mundane reason of providing a generation of boys who could become civil servants, legal administrators, secretaries to politicians, but the brightest, most imaginative boys were inspired by what they learned of the techniques of language and the literature of the ancient world, inspired to become poets and dramatists themselves, not to become civil servants. And so the flowering of the theatre and poetry in the last 20 years of Queen Elizabeth’s reign really was precisely to do with that educational revolution. Shakespeare as a poet, as a playwright, really began in school.\nInterviewer: Shakespeare’s achievement was to show just how rich a language English could be and, along with the publication of the English Bible, Shakespeare played the greatest part in creating the language we speak today.",
