   "transcript": "Interviewer: Today, on Word of Mouth, we’re going to be talking about emoji, those little characters that seem to be everywhere nowadays, peppering our text messages and social media posts with little pictures. With me in the studio as always is Dr Laura Wright of the University of Cambridge.\nLaura Wright: Hello, Michael.\nInterviewer: And we’re joined today by language and communication expert, Professor Vyv Evans, author of the forthcoming book, The Emoji Code. Vyv – waving hands, smiley face, champagne, popping hands, clapping, thumbs up – welcome!\nVyv Evans: Thank you very much.\nInterviewer: You’ve been (re)searching emoji, yes? Why do you find them so interesting?\nVyv Evans: They are incredibly interesting. I think the main reason is that they enable us to express emotion and empathy in digital communication. Increasingly what we’re finding is that digital communication is taking over from certain aspects of face-to-face interaction. In the UK today, for example, adults spend 22 hours online on average each week. One of the reasons emoji is so interesting is they really do enable us to express our emotional selves much more effectively.\nInterviewer: Much more effectively than the written word, you’re saying?\nVyv Evans: Well, the written word is the way in which we communicate digitally but one of the things that we find, I think, and you’ll probably recognise this, is what I refer to as the angry jerk phenomenon. This is when you receive an email from someone you know to be otherwise calm and sane but they sound as if they’re bonkers, they’re raving mad. One of the problems is in text, in digital communication, is that text itself sucks the empathy out of the message.\nInterviewer: So, for example, if there’s a dog barking outside, I’m really angry that the dog’s barking outside, can’t I say ‘I’m really angry, there’s a dog barking outside’?\nVyv Evans: Well, you can indeed, but one of the things that we’re able to do now because we’re looking at one another in the studio, is we’re able to look at each other’s gaze. We can also hear tone of voice, and these are powerful cues in terms of emotional expression. By one estimate, between 60 and 70% of social meaning in face-to-face spoken interaction derives from eye gaze –\nLaura Wright: Eyebrow waggling.\nVyv Evans: Gesture, indeed.\nInterviewer: Ear waggling in my case.\nVyv Evans: Prosody.\nLaura Wright: Lip chewing.\nVyv Evans: Intonation.\nLaura Wright: Ear hugging.\nVyv Evans: Indeed, and this is completely missing when we write text.\nInterviewer: But can’t I write ‘I’m really angry, the dog is barking’? ‘Really angry’, doesn’t that say that I’m really angry?\nVyv Evans: It does indeed, but what we also know is that people often use emotional cues from speech prosody which trumps what people say.\nInterviewer: What’s speech prosody?\nVyv Evans: So speech prosody has to do with pitch contour, tempo, intonation. Let me give you an example of how intonation can change the meaning of the words. So take a bog standard example like ‘I love you’. So if you say that with falling pitch as if it’s a sort of a statement, ‘I love you’, you can intone it.\nInterviewer: ‘I love you.’\nVyv Evans: But it’s a statement of undying love presumably. Now if you switch the pitch contour, you change the intonational pattern with rising pitch so that it’s stated as a question –\nInterviewer and\nVyv Evans: ‘I love you?’\nVyv Evans: It becomes a derisive counterblast.\nInterviewer: Oh, it’s ironic.\nVyv Evans: It can, it becomes ironic.\nInterviewer: ‘You think I love you?’\nLaura Wright: Don’t do this one to Emma.\nInterviewer: No.\nVyv Evans: So the point here is that the words themselves can lie. When we’re speaking to one another, we pick (up) on these non-verbal cues so this is speech prosody known as para-language and we, we draw conclusions about whether people are telling the truth or not, so you can in fact lie with your words and it’s less easy to lie with your face or with your intonation.",
